Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 1 Maximum Likelihood Estimation In the previous lecture we have seen that for the exponential model, the parameter value which maximises the likelihood is the one which ‘best’ explains the data. In this lecture we will make this idea into the main estimation method of the module: we define the maximum likelihood estimator, practise computing it for the families from tables 1.1 and 1.2, and will see problems where more thought is needed. We will see that the estimator is not unbiased in general, but is invariant under reparametrisation; the deeper justification of the method will be given in lectures 13, 16 and 17. 1.1 The Maximum Likelihood Estimator Example 4.5 already contains the idea: for the exponential sample the log-likelihood increases up to 	heta=1/ ar{x} and then decreases, so this value fits the data best. We now formalise this principle. Definition 1.1. A maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) for  	heta is an estimator \hat{	heta}=\hat{	heta}(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) , which maximises the likelihood function over the parameter space, i.e. which satisfies L(\hat{	heta})\geq L(	heta)\qquad	ext{for all $	heta\in\Theta$.} Since the logarithm is strictly increasing, \hat{	heta} maximises  L if and only if it maximises the log-likelihood  \ell and for the reasons given in lecture 4 we mostly work with  \ell . The definition is careful to avoid the problem of a maximum not existing or not being unique. For example, the likelihood could increase as the edge of the parameter space is approached, so that no maximum exists. Similarly, the maximum may not be unique. In such cases, any maximiser can be used. For the standard families of models and typical data, neither of these problems will occur and it will be safe to speak of “the” MLE. If the set \Theta is an interval and the log-likelihood function is differentiable, then calculus can be used to find the MLE: at a maximum inside the interval  \Theta , the derivative of  \ell equals zero and thus the MLE satisfies the likelihood equation \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0, i.e. the MLE is a zero of the score function from definition 4.4. The general procedure is to find the log-likelihood function, to solve the likelihood equation and to verify that the solution is a maximum, for example by checking that \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta)<0 or by checking that the score changes from positive to negative. Warning. The solution of the likelihood equation only gives interior critical points. These could be minima or inflection points and the checking step is required to avoid wrong answers. Also, the above procedure assumes that the maximum is interior and that \ell is differentiable. We will see that these assumptions can be violated. 1.2 Worked Examples We start our analysis by considering the MLE for commonly used families of probability distributions. For each of them we find the MLE using the recipe from the previous section, and then we check whether the resulting estimator is unbiased. The Exponential Distribution The exponential distribution is the simplest of the three cases considered here: the likelihood equation has a single root and the moments of the MLE can be computed exactly. Example 1.2. From examples 4.3 and 4.5 we know that the score function of an exponential sample x_{1},\dots,x_{n} is given by \ell^{\prime}(	heta)= rac{n}{	heta}-\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i} for all 	heta>0 . Setting \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0 gives n/	heta=\sum_{i}x_{i} , and thus the likelihood equation has the unique solution 	heta= rac{n}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}}= rac{1}{ ar{x}}. To check that this root is a maximum, we differentiate once more and find \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta)=- rac{n}{	heta^{2}}<0 for all 	heta>0 . Thus, the function \ell is strictly concave and the root 1/ ar{x} is the only global maximum. We conclude that the MLE for the rate of the exponential distribution is \hat{	heta}= rac{1}{ ar{X}}. This coincides with the method of moments estimator from example 4.7. To find the bias of this estimator, let T=X_{1}+\dots+X_{n} , so that \hat{	heta}=n/T . In exercise 2.3 we found, using the gamma density of  T , that \mathbb{E}_{	heta}(\hat{	heta})=n	heta/(n-1) for n\geq 2 , while for n=1 the expectation is infinite. Thus, for n\geq 2 , the MLE is biased with bias \mathop{\mathrm{bias}}
olimits(\hat{	heta})=\mathbb{E}_{	heta}(\hat{	heta}% )-	heta= rac{n	heta}{n-1}-	heta= rac{	heta}{n-1}. Thus, the MLE for the rate is biased but asymptotically unbiased in the sense of definition 2.4. Multiplying \hat{	heta} by (n-1)/n removes the bias: the estimator 	ilde{	heta}=(n-1)\hat{	heta}/n=(n-1)/T satisfies \mathbb{E}_{	heta}(	ilde{	heta})=	heta . The Bernoulli Distribution For the Bernoulli distribution the recipe works whenever the data contain both successes and failures. The remaining two cases need separate treatment. Example 1.3. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be an i.i.d. Bernoulli sample with success probability  	heta , and let s=\sum_{i}x_{i} be the observed number of successes. As in example 4.2 we have the likelihood L(	heta)=	heta^{s}(1-	heta)^{n-s} and thus \ell(	heta)=s\log	heta+(n-s)\log(1-	heta) for all 	heta\in(0,1) . Assume 0<s<n . Then the likelihood equation is 0=\ell^{\prime}(	heta)= rac{s}{	heta}- rac{n-s}{1-	heta}. This equation implies s(1-	heta)=(n-s)	heta and the only solution to this equation is 	heta=s/n . Since \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta)=-s/	heta^{2}-(n-s)/(1-	heta)^{2}<0 everywhere, this root is the global maximum and the MLE for the proportion of successes is \hat{	heta}= rac{s}{n}= ar{X}. Since \mathbb{E}_{	heta}( ar{X})=	heta , the MLE for the Bernoulli parameter is unbiased. Remark. The two cases s=0 and s=n are special. For s=0 the function \ell(	heta)=n\log(1-	heta) is strictly decreasing, and for s=n the function \ell(	heta)=n\log	heta is strictly increasing. In both cases the supremum is approached at the respective boundary point. We cannot find these points by setting \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0 . Additionally, in this module we use the open interval \Theta=(0,1) as the parameter space, so for us no 	heta\in\Theta maximises the log-likelihood and if we are pedantic the MLE does not exist. The Normal Distribution The normal distribution with both parameters unknown has a two-dimensional parameter vector, and thus the likelihood equation becomes a system of two equations. Example 1.4. Consider X_{1},\dots,X_{n}\sim N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) , where both parameters are unknown. Then we have 	heta=(\mu,\sigma^{2}) . Taking logarithms we get \ell(\mu,\sigma^{2})=- rac{n}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^{2})- rac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}% \sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i}-\mu)^{2}. To find the maximum, we set both partial derivatives equal to zero. The partial derivatives are  rac{\partial\ell}{\partial\mu}= rac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i}-\mu) and  rac{\partial\ell}{\partial\sigma^{2}}=- rac{n}{2\sigma^{2}}+ rac{1}{2% \sigma^{4}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i}-\mu)^{2}. The first derivative is zero only for \mu= ar{x} . Substituting this value into the second derivative and setting it to zero gives \sigma^{2}=(1/n)\sum_{i}(x_{i}- ar{x})^{2} . To check that this solution is the maximum, we note that for every fixed  \sigma^{2} the sum of squares \sum_{i}(x_{i}-\mu)^{2} is smallest for \mu= ar{x} , and thus \ell(\mu,\sigma^{2})\leq\ell( ar{x},\sigma^{2}) . At \mu= ar{x} the derivative with respect to  \sigma^{2} can be written as  igl{(}\sum_{i}(x_{i}- ar{x})^{2}-n\sigma^{2} igr{)}/(2\sigma^{4}) , which is positive to the left of the solution and negative to the right. Thus, the MLE is the pair \hat{\mu}= ar{X}\qquad	ext{and}\qquad\hat{\sigma}^{2}= rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^% {n}(X_{i}- ar{X})^{2}. The MLE \hat{\mu}= ar{X} for the mean is unbiased. The MLE \hat{\sigma}^{2} for the variance is the estimator from example 2.5, where we found the bias -\sigma^{2}/n . Thus, \hat{\sigma}^{2} is biased but asymptotically unbiased. In all three examples considered in this section, the MLE is either unbiased or asymptotically unbiased. Note that there are other examples, where this property does not hold. The behaviour of the MLE for large samples in general is the subject of lectures 16 and 17. 1.3 When the Recipe Fails The recipe for finding the MLE can fail in three different ways: the maximum may sit at a point where the log-likelihood is not differentiable, the likelihood equation may have several roots, and the likelihood equation may have no closed-form solution. We consider these three cases in turn. A Maximum at the Boundary Both assumptions in the recipe, i.e. an interior maximum and differentiable log-likelihood, break down for the uniform model. In this case we have to return to definition 1.1 and maximise the expression directly. Example 1.5. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n}\sim	ext{Uniform}(0,	heta) be i.i.d., as in example 1.2. From the joint density found in exercise 1.3, the likelihood is L(	heta)= egin{cases}	heta^{-n}&	ext{if $	heta\geq\max_{i}x_{i}$,}\ 0&	ext{otherwise.}\end{cases} Calculus is useless here: on the region where L is positive its derivative -n	heta^{-n-1} is negative everywhere, so the likelihood equation has no solution, and at 	heta=\max_{i}x_{i} the function is not even continuous. But maximising directly is easy: the likelihood is zero for 	heta<\max_{i}x_{i} and strictly decreasing for 	heta\geq\max_{i}x_{i} , and thus largest at the left edge of the positive region. The MLE is the sample maximum, \hat{	heta}=\max_{i}X_{i}. This identifies the estimator we have been tracking since exercise 1.3, which by exercise 4.4 beats the method of moments estimator 2 ar{X} in mean squared error for every n\geq 3 . The likelihood also rules out impossible parameter values automatically: for the dataset x_{1}=0.1 , x_{2}=0.2 , x_{3}=0.9 of lecture 4, any 	heta<0.9 has likelihood zero, so the contradiction the method of moments overlooked cannot arise. Several Roots The uniform model fails the recipe in an obvious way. A failure which is harder to spot is a likelihood equation with several roots. Consider the t location model with 
