eneral case in this module. As an application, we find the MLE for the median waiting time in the exponential model without any further maximisation. Example 1.7. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. exponential with rate  	heta>0 . The median waiting time is the value  m such that \mathbb{P}_{	heta}(X_{1}\leq m)=1/2 . Since \mathbb{P}_{	heta}(X_{1}\leq m)=1-e^{-	heta m} , this condition is equivalent to e^{-	heta m}=1/2 , and taking logarithms gives m=g(	heta)= rac{\log 2}{	heta}. The map  g is strictly decreasing on (0,\infty) , with g(	heta)	o\infty as 	heta	o 0 and g(	heta)	o 0 as 	heta	o\infty , and thus it maps (0,\infty) bijectively onto itself. Since the MLE for the rate is \hat{	heta}=1/ ar{X} by example 1.2, proposition 1.6 shows that the MLE for the median is \hat{m}=g(\hat{	heta})= rac{\log 2}{\hat{	heta}}=(\log 2)\, ar{X}. Exercise 1.8 shows that this example contains a surprise: \hat{m} is unbiased, despite the MLE for the rate being biased. In general, unbiasedness is lost under nonlinear reparametrisation, and thus there is no analogue of proposition 1.6 for unbiasedness. Since we have seen in this lecture that the MLE can be biased, non-unique and even non-existent, we need to do more work to understand the method. The arguments for the validity of the MLE can be found in lectures 13, 16 and 17. Summary. •​The maximum likelihood estimator \hat{	heta} is the parameter value which maximises the likelihood (or log-likelihood) of the data. •​For smooth problems, where the maximum is an interior point of the parameter space, the MLE can be found by solving the likelihood equation \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0 and verifying that the root is a maximum. •​If this recipe fails, the definition can still be used: for the uniform distribution the maximum sits at the boundary, at \hat{	heta}=\max_{i}X_{i} , and for the t location model with two observations far apart the MLE is not unique. •​The MLE can be biased. •​The MLE is invariant under reparametrisation: the MLE for 