eq 0 . Finally, since the density must integrate (or sum) to one, the function  B is determined by the other three functions: we have equation (3.1) (3.1) B(	heta)=\log\int h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)\,T(x) igr{)}\,\mathrm{d}x. These rewritings give the following examples. Example 3.2. The Bernoulli distribution with 	heta\in(0,1) is a one-parameter exponential family with h(x)=1 for x\in\{0,1\} and h(x)=0 otherwise, natural statistic T(x)=x , natural parameter \eta(	heta)=\log igl{(}	heta/(1-	heta) igr{)} , the log-odds of success, and B(	heta)=-\log(1-	heta) . Example 3.3. The Poisson distribution with 	heta>0 is a one-parameter exponential family with h(x)=1/x! for x\in\mathbb{N}_{0} and h(x)=0 otherwise, natural statistic T(x)=x , natural parameter \eta(	heta)=\log	heta and B(	heta)=	heta . Example 3.4. The exponential distribution with rate 	heta>0 is a one-parameter exponential family with h(x)=1 for x\geq 0 and h(x)=0 otherwise, natural statistic T(x)=x , natural parameter \eta(	heta)=-	heta and B(	heta)=-\log	heta . The normal distribution is also an exponential family, but since both parameters are unknown, we need to use the two-parameter version of the definition from section 3.2. Remark. The uniform distribution on [0,	heta] is not an exponential family, since the support of the distribution changes with the parameter. In contrast, the support of an exponential family is always fixed. This is the same reason why the likelihood equation was useless in example 1.5. Similarly, the t location model from section 1.3 is not an exponential family, despite the fact that its support is all of  \mathbb{R} ; we do not prove this. The shape survives the passage from one observation to a sample. Lemma 3.5. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. from the one-parameter exponential family in definition 3.1. Then the joint density of the sample is f(x_{1},\dots,x_{n};	heta)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}h(x_{i})\cdot\exp\Bigl{(}\eta(% 	heta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}T(x_{i})-nB(	heta)\Bigr{)}, which is of the form of definition 3.1 with the same natural parameter  \eta(	heta) , natural statistic T_{n}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}T(X_{i}) , the function \prod_{i}h(x_{i}) instead of  h and nB(	heta) instead of  B(	heta) . Proof. Since the observations are independent, the joint density is the product \prod_{i=1}^{n}h(x_{i})\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)\,T(x_{i})-B(	heta) igr{)} of the marginal densities. Combining the n exponents using the rule e^{a}e^{b}=e^{a+b} gives the required form. This completes the proof. ∎ Read as a function of  	heta , the joint density is the likelihood, and thus the log-likelihood of an exponential family sample is equation (3.2) (3.2) \ell(	heta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log h(x_{i})+\eta(	heta)\,T_{n}-nB(	heta). The first term does not involve  	heta . Thus, up to a factor which does not depend on  	heta , the likelihood depends on the data only through the single number T_{n}=\sum_{i}T(x_{i}) . This is an extension of example 4.3 to the whole class; in lecture 4 we will see in which sense the value T_{n} contains all the information about  	heta . 3.2 Several Parameters The normal distribution where both \mu and \sigma^{2} are unknown does not fit definition 3.1, since the exponent contains the two products x\mu/\sigma^{2} and -x^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}) , which cannot be combined into a single product \eta(	heta)\,T(x) . The extension is to allow a sum of k such products in the exponent. Definition 3.6. A parametric model  igl{(}f(x;	heta)\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!	heta\in\Theta igr{)} is a k -parameter exponential family, if the density or the probability weights can be written as f(x;	heta)=h(x)\exp\Bigl{(}\sum_{j=1}^{k}\eta_{j}(	heta)\,T_{j}(x)-B(	heta)% \Bigr{)} for all  x and all 	heta\in\Theta , where h\geq 0 and T_{1},\dots,T_{k} are functions of  x alone and \eta_{1},\dots,\eta_{k} and  B are functions of  	heta alone. The vector  igl{(}T_{1}(X),\dots,T_{k}(X) igr{)} is the natural statistic and  igl{(}\eta_{1}(	heta),\dots,\eta_{k}(	heta) igr{)} is the natural parameter of the family. The number  k counts the products in the exponent, not the components of  	heta . In most of our examples the two agree, but they do not have to: the normal distribution N(	heta,	heta^{2}) considered below has the single parameter 	heta>0 , but its exponent needs two products. An exponential family in which k is larger than the dimension of  \Theta is called a curved exponential family. The support of the distribution is again \{x\mid h(x)>0\} and the proof of lemma 3.5 applies without change: an i.i.d. sample is again a k -parameter exponential family, where the natural statistic is the vector of the k sums \sum_{i}T_{j}(X_{i}) . Example 3.7. By expanding the square in the exponent of the density of the normal distribution N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) from table 1.2, we can write f(x;\mu,\sigma^{2})= rac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}\exp\Bigl{(}- rac{x^{2}}{2% \sigma^{2}}+ rac{\mu}{\sigma^{2}}\,x- rac{\mu^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Bigr{)}. If only \mu is unknown, the factor (2\pi\sigma^{2})^{-1/2} and the term -x^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}) contain no unknown quantities and we can define the function h(x)=(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{-1/2}e^{-x^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})} : we have found a one-parameter exponential family with natural statistic T(x)=x , natural parameter \eta(\mu)=\mu/\sigma^{2} and B(\mu)=\mu^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}) . If both parameters are unknown, we have to interpret the term -x^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}) as a second product and we get a two-parameter exponential family with h(x)=1 , natural statistic (x,x^{2}) , natural parameter  igl{(}\mu/\sigma^{2},\,-1/(2\sigma^{2}) igr{)} and B(	heta)=\mu^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})+ rac{1}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^{2}) . For a sample, the natural statistic is  igl{(}\sum_{i}x_{i},\sum_{i}x_{i}^{2} igr{)} . Since \sum_{i}(x_{i}- ar{x})^{2}=\sum_{i}x_{i}^{2}-n ar{x}^{2} , for fixed  n this pair and the pair  igl{(} ar{x},\sum_{i}(x_{i}- ar{x})^{2} igr{)} used to derive the maximum likelihood estimator in example 1.4 are one-to-one functions of each other. The gamma distribution with both parameters unknown is a second example of a two-parameter exponential family. If we write x^{\alpha-1}=e^{(\alpha-1)\log x} , then the natural statistic is (\log x,\,x) and the details are worked out in exercise 3.1. For a sample of size  n , the natural statistic is the pair  igl{(}\sum_{i}\log x_{i},\sum_{i}x_{i} igr{)} , which is different from the pair of moments used in example 4.9. For the completeness statements in lecture 6 it is important that the natural parameter varies in as many directions as there are natural statistics. Mathematically, this can be expressed as the following condition. Definition 3.8. A one-parameter exponential family is of full rank, if the set \eta(\Theta)=\{\eta(	heta)\mid	heta\in\Theta\} of natural parameter values contains a non-degenerate open interval. A k -parameter exponential family is of full rank, if the set  igl{\{} igl{(}\eta_{1}(	heta),\dots,\eta_{k}(	heta) igr{)}\mathrel{ ig{% |}}	heta\in\Theta igr{\}} contains a non-empty open subset of  \mathbb{R}^{k} . In the one-parameter case, the condition is almost always satisfied, since the image of an interval  \Theta under a continuous, non-constant  \eta is an interval with more than one point; all the one-parameter examples above are of full rank. For several parameters, the condition can be more interesting: the natural parameter values of the normal distribution fill the open half-plane \{(\eta_{1},\eta_{2})\mid\eta_{2}<0\} (example 3.7) and the natural parameter values of the gamma distribution fill an open quadrant (exercise 3.1), but the natural parameter values of N(	heta,	heta^{2}) with 	heta>0 , where the standard deviation equals the mean, form a curve in  \mathbb{R}^{2} which does not contain an open set, and thus this curved exponential family is not of full rank (exercise 3.2). 3.3 The Natural Parametrisation and the Cumulant Function We return to one-parameter exponential families. Since the parameter enters the density only via \eta(	heta) , it is often convenient to use  \eta as the parameter. In this case, the normalising function (3.1) deserves a name, since its first two derivatives are the mean and the variance of the natural statistic. Definition 3.9. Let h and T be as in definition 3.1. Then the natural parametrisation of the family is given by f(x;\eta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta\,T(x)-K(\eta) igr{)},\qquad\eta\in\mathcal{N}. Here the cumulant function (also called the log-partition function) is given by K(\eta)=\log\int h(x)\,e^{\eta T(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x, with a sum in place of the integral for discrete models. The natural parameter space is the set \mathcal{N}=\{\eta\in\mathbb{R}\mid K(\eta)<\infty\} of all  \eta for which the integral is finite. Since K makes f(x;\eta) integrate to one, the formula defines a probability distribution for every \eta\in\mathcal{N} , whether or not \eta is of the form \eta(	heta) ; comparing with (3.1) shows that \eta(	heta)\in\mathcal{N} and B(	heta)=K igl{(}\eta(	heta) igr{)} , so that the original family sits inside this larger one. For the three examples of section 3.1 we find K(\eta)=\log(1+e^{\eta}) with \mathcal{N}=\mathbb{R} for the Bernoulli distribution, K(\eta)=e^{\eta} with \mathcal{N}=\mathbb{R} for the Poisson distribution and K(\eta)=-\log(-\eta) with \mathcal{N}=(-\infty,0) for the exponential distribution. In all three cases \mathcal{N} is an open interval and 	heta\mapsto\eta(	heta) is a bijection onto  \mathcal{N} , making the natural parametrisation a relabelling in the sense of proposition 1.6. The first two derivatives of  K give the mean and the variance of  T(X) , and the name of  K comes from a related object. The moment generating function of a random variable  Y is the function s\mapsto\mathbb{E}(e^{sY}) , defined for all  s for which the expectation is finite. Its logarithm s\mapsto\log\mathbb{E}(e^{sY}) is called the cumulant generating function of  Y , and the derivatives of this function at s=0 are called the cumulants of  Y . The first two cumulants are the mean and the variance of  Y . For a random variable  Y with values in  \mathbb{N}_{0} the closely related probability generating function is given by equation (3.3) (3.3) G_{Y}(z)=\mathbb{E} igl{(}z^{Y} igr{)}=\sum_{y=0}^{\infty}\mathbb{P}(Y=y)\,z% ^{y}, defined for all z\geq 0 for which the sum is finite. Substituting z=e^{s} shows that s\mapsto G_{Y}(e^{s}) is the moment generating function of  Y . In exercise 3.6 we show that s\mapsto K(\eta+s)-K(\eta) is the cumulant generating function of  T(X) , which explains the name of  K . Lemma 3.10. Let \eta be an interior point of the natural parameter space  \mathcal{N} . Then, in the natural parametrisation, \mathbb{E}_{\eta} igl{(}T(X) igr{)}=K^{\prime}(\eta)\qquad	ext{and}\qquad% \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{\eta} igl{(}T(X) igr{)}=K^{\prime\prime}(% \eta). Proof. Set Z(\eta)=\int h(x)e^{\eta T(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x , so that K=\log Z . Since the density f(x;	heta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)\,T(x)-B(	heta) igr{)} integrates to one, the function h\geq 0 is not identically zero. Together with e^{\eta T(x)}>0 this gives Z(\eta)>0 , and thus we may divide by  Z(\eta) below. Since \eta is in the interior of  \mathcal{N} , we can take derivatives inside the integral as many times as required; this is a result from analysis which we use without proof. Taking derivatives once, we get Z^{\prime}(\eta)=\int T(x)\,h(x)\,e^{\eta T(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x. Dividing by Z(\eta)=e^{K(\eta)} changes the integrand to T(x) times the density f(x;\eta) and thus we get K^{\prime}(\eta)= rac{Z^{\prime}(\eta)}{Z(\eta)}=\int T(x)\,h(x)\,e^{\eta T(x% )-K(\eta)}\,\mathrm{d}x=\mathbb{E}_{\eta} igl{(}T(X) igr{)}. Taking derivatives again, we find K^{\prime\prime}(\eta)= rac{Z^{\prime\prime}(\eta)}{Z(\eta)}-\Bigl{(} rac{Z^% {\prime}(\eta)}{Z(\eta)}\Bigr{)}^{2}, and using the same argument as above, with T(x)^{2} in the integrand, we find Z^{\prime\prime}(\eta)/Z(\eta)=\mathbb{E}_{\eta} igl{(}T(X)^{2} igr{)} . Thus we have K^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=\mathbb{E}_{\eta}(T(X)^{2})-\mathbb{E}_{\eta}(T(X))^{2}% =\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
