olimits_{\eta}(T_{n})=nK^{\prime\prime}(\eta) . 3.4 What the Structure Buys We conclude this lecture by collecting what the exponential family shape tells us about the questions of lectures 4 and 1, and by indicating where the shape will be used again. The first consequence concerns the likelihood equation. By lemma 3.5, written in the natural parametrisation, the log-likelihood of a sample is \ell(\eta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log h(x_{i})+\eta\,T_{n}-nK(\eta), and thus we have \ell^{\prime}(\eta)=T_{n}-nK^{\prime}(\eta) and, by lemma 3.10, \ell^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=-nK^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=-n\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}% 
olimits_{\eta} igl{(}T(X) igr{)} . If T(X) is not constant, this variance is positive and \ell is strictly concave on the interior of  \mathcal{N} . Since \ell^{\prime} is then strictly decreasing, the likelihood equation \ell^{\prime}(\eta)=0 has at most one root, and if there is a root, it is the maximum likelihood estimator for  \eta . When 	heta\mapsto\eta(	heta) is a bijection, proposition 1.6 transfers this estimator back to  	heta . The argument shows uniqueness, but not existence: for a Bernoulli sample with no successes we have T_{n}=0 , and the equation K^{\prime}(\eta)=e^{\eta}/(1+e^{\eta})=0 has no solution. This is the case s=0 from the remark after example 1.3. The second consequence concerns smoothness. Many of the results later in the module are proved by differentiating with respect to the parameter, and they require the support of the distribution not to depend on  	heta , the density to be smooth in  	heta and derivatives to be taken inside integrals. For an exponential family the support is fixed by definition, the density is smooth in  	heta whenever \eta and  B are, and the argument in the proof of lemma 3.10 allows differentiation under the integral sign whenever \eta(	heta) lies in the interior of  \mathcal{N} . Similarly, if \eta is one-to-one and T(X) is not constant, then different values of  	heta give different distributions, and the parameter is identifiable in the sense of definition 4.10. Under these mild conditions on  \eta , the exponential families are the standard examples for the theory in the rest of the module. Finally, the observation that the likelihood depends on the data only through T_{n} and  n suggests that, once T_{n} is known, the individual observations carry no further information about  	heta . In lecture 4 we make this idea precise, and lemma 3.5 will do most of the work there. From then on, the natural statistic  T_{n} will be the starting point whenever we look for good estimators, and again when we update our knowledge about  	heta using the Bayesian approach in the second half of the module. Summary. •​A one-parameter exponential family has density of the form f(x;	heta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)T(x)-B(	heta) igr{)} , where the natural statistic is  T , the natural parameter is  \eta and the support of the distribution does not depend on  	heta . Examples of exponential families include the Bernoulli, Poisson, exponential, normal and gamma distributions. The uniform distribution is not an exponential family. •​An i.i.d. sample from an exponential family is again an exponential family. In this case, the natural statistic is T_{n}=\sum_{i}T(X_{i}) and the likelihood depends on the data only through  T_{n} . •​A k -parameter exponential family is of full rank, if the set of natural parameter values contains a non-empty open subset of  \mathbb{R}^{k} . This condition is required for completeness in lecture 6. The curved exponential family N(	heta,	heta^{2}) is not of full rank. •​In the natural parametrisation f(x;\eta)=h(x)\exp(\eta T(x)-K(\eta)) the cumulant function K can be used to find the mean and variance of the natural statistic: we have \mathbb{E}_{\eta}(T(X))=K^{\prime}(\eta) and \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{\eta}(T(X))=K^{\prime\prime}(\eta) . •​If T(X) is not constant, the log-likelihood is strictly concave in the natural parameter, and thus the likelihood equation has at most one root. A root need not exist. •​Under mild conditions on  \eta , exponential families satisfy the smoothness conditions of the later theory. Exercise 3.1. Let X be gamma distributed, X\sim	ext{Gamma}(\alpha, eta) with shape \alpha>0 and rate   eta>0 , both unknown, i.e. with density f(x;\alpha, eta)= eta^{\alpha}x^{\alpha-1}e^{- eta x}/\Gamma(\alpha) for x>0 , as listed in table A.2. 1.​Show that this model is a two-parameter exponential family, as given in definition 3.6, with h(x)=1 for x>0 and h(x)=0 otherwise, natural statistic (\log x,\,x) , natural parameter (\alpha-1,\,- eta) , and function B(\alpha, eta)=\log\Gamma(\alpha)-\alpha\log eta . 2.​Show that the set of natural parameter values is the open quadrant \{(\eta_{1},\eta_{2})\mid\eta_{1}>-1,\ \eta_{2}<0\} and thus the family has full rank as given in definition 3.8. 3.​Assume that the shape  \alpha is known, as in exercise 1.2. Show that then the factor x^{\alpha-1} can be incorporated into the function  h to yield a one-parameter exponential family with natural statistic T(x)=x and natural parameter - eta . Exercise 3.2. Consider the normal distribution N(	heta,	heta^{2}) with mean and standard deviation both equal to  	heta>0 . 1.​Using example 3.7, show that this model is a curved two-parameter exponential family with natural statistic and natural parameter given by T(x)=(x,x^{2})\qquad	ext{and}\qquad\eta(	heta)= igl{(}1/	heta,\,-1/(2% 	heta^{2}) igr{)}. 2.​Show that as 	heta varies, the natural parameter values follow the curve \eta_{2}=-\eta_{1}^{2}/2 with \eta_{1}>0 . Conclude that this family is not of full rank in the sense of definition 3.8. Exercise 3.3. Let X be geometrically distributed with parameter p\in(0,1) , i.e.  f(x;p)=(1-p)^{x-1}p for x\in\mathbb{N} , as in exercises 4.2 and 1.1. 1.​Show that this dist