Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 4 Sufficiency In example 4.2 we have observed ten coin tosses and got seven heads. Writing 	heta for the heads probability, the likelihood in this example is L(	heta)=	heta^{7}(1-	heta)^{3} . This function depends on the data only via the number  7 of heads, but not on the order of heads and tails, i.e. for learning about  	heta we can replace the full record of observations by this single number. This lecture makes the idea precise: a sufficient statistic contains all information about the parameter. The factorisation theorem helps to find sufficient statistics, and the concept of sufficiency forms the basis of improvements of estimators in lecture 6. In this lecture we write X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) for the data, x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{n}) for an observed value, and f(x;	heta)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}f(x_{i};	heta) for the joint density or joint probability weights of the sample; the vector argument distinguishes it from the density of a single observation. As a function of  	heta for fixed data, this is the likelihood L(	heta) from lecture 4. 4.1 Sufficient Statistics A statistic T=T(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) in the sense of definition 1.3 reduces the data to a smaller summary, and usually some information is lost in the process. The following definition expresses that no information about  	heta is lost. Definition 4.1. A statistic T=T(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) is sufficient for  	heta , if the conditional distribution of (X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) given the value T=t does not depend on  	heta , for all values  t of T . If T is sufficient, anyone who knows the value  t of  T can generate a new data set with the same distribution as the original data set, by sampling from the conditional distribution of  X given T=t . Since this distribution does not depend on  	heta , all information about  	heta that we could obtain by studying the data set can now be obtained by studying  t alone. Example 4.2. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Bernoulli with success probability  p and let T=\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i} be the number of successes. We know that T\sim	ext{Binomial}(n,p) , as a sum of n Bernoulli variables. Let t\in\{0,1,\dots,n\} and let x\in\{0,1\}^{n} be a data vector with \sum_{i}x_{i}=t . Since the event \{X=x\} is contained in the event \{T=t\} , we have \mathbb{P}_{p}(X=x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0muT=t)= rac{\mathbb{P}_{p}(X=x)}{% \mathbb{P}_{p}(T=t)}= rac{p^{t}(1-p)^{n-t}}{ inom{n}{t}p^{t}(1-p)^{n-t}}=%  rac{1}{ inom{n}{t}}. If \sum_{i}x_{i}
eq t , the events \{X=x\} and \{T=t\} are disjoint and the conditional probability is zero. In both cases the value does not depend on  p , and thus T is sufficient for  p . Given the number of successes, each of the  inom{n}{t} possible arrangements of successes and failures is equally likely, whatever the value of  p : the positions of the successes do not contain any information about the coin. Two simple observations follow from the definition. First, the whole sample T=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) is always sufficient, since given the whole sample there is no randomness left. Secondly, if \varphi is a one-to-one function, the events \{\varphi(T)=\varphi(t)\} and \{T=t\} coincide, and thus \varphi(T) is sufficient whenever T is: a one-to-one function of a sufficient statistic is sufficient again. For the coin tosses the sample mean  ar{X}=T/n is thus sufficient too. 4.2 The Factorisation Theorem Directly verifying the definition of sufficiency requires knowledge of the distribution of  T and involves a conditional probability which, for most models, is tedious to compute. The following proposition replaces the computation with a condition on the density or weights. Proposition 4.3 (Fisher–Neyman factorisation theorem). The statistic T is sufficient for  	heta , if and only if there are functions h and  g such that equation (4.1) (4.1) f(x;	heta)=h(x)\,g igl{(}T(x);	heta igr{)} for all data vectors  x and all 	heta\in\Theta . The function  h may not depend on  	heta at all, and the function  g may depend on the data only through  T(x) : the parameter and the data interact only through  T . Nothing in the statement requires T or  	heta to be one-dimensional. We give a proof for discrete data only. Proof for discrete data. Since we have discrete observations, the joint probability weight f(x;	heta)=\mathbb{P}_{	heta}(X=x) is the probability of observing the data vector  x . Assume first that T is sufficient and let x be a data vector with t=T(x) . Since the event \{X=x\} is